WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.119
Sometimes one query is not enough.

00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:08.238
You want users who listened to pop, plus users who listened to rock.

00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:11.357
Or users who did both.

00:00:11.357 --> 00:00:14.477
Or pop but not rock.

00:00:14.477 --> 00:00:17.596
These are set operations.

00:00:17.596 --> 00:00:20.715
SQL has four of them, and each combines two queries into one result.

00:00:20.715 --> 00:00:24.482
Start with two query results.

00:00:24.482 --> 00:00:28.249
Query A, the pop listeners: Mickey and Minnie.

00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:32.015
Query B, the rock listeners: Mickey and Daffy.

00:00:32.015 --> 00:00:35.782
Mickey is in both.

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:39.549
Now combine them four ways.

00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:43.316
UNION takes either side and removes duplicates: Mickey, Minnie, Daffy, three rows.

00:00:43.316 --> 00:00:47.082
UNION ALL keeps the duplicate, so Mickey appears twice, four rows.

00:00:47.082 --> 00:00:50.849
INTERSECT keeps only the rows in both: just Mickey.

00:00:50.849 --> 00:00:54.616
And EXCEPT, A minus B, keeps what is in A but not in B: just Minnie.

00:00:54.616 --> 00:00:58.672
One catch.

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:02.728
SQL tables are not really sets; they allow duplicates, like real life.

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:06.784
Mathematicians call that a multiset, or a bag.

00:01:06.784 --> 00:01:10.839
The plain operators, UNION, INTERSECT, and EXCEPT, impose set behavior and drop duplicates.

00:01:10.839 --> 00:01:14.895
Their ALL variants keep the bag-ness.

00:01:14.895 --> 00:01:18.951
Pick the wrong one and your row counts change in ways that are easy to miss.

00:01:18.951 --> 00:01:23.525
Four operators, two modes each.

00:01:23.525 --> 00:01:28.099
The plain modes act on sets and drop duplicates; the ALL modes act on bags and keep them.

00:01:28.099 --> 00:01:32.674
UNION to add, INTERSECT for overlap, EXCEPT for the difference.

00:01:32.674 --> 00:01:37.248
Always pause to ask: do I want the duplicates removed, or not?
